  "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y=0\\,\u21d2\\,y=-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\text{\u00a0}0,\\text{\u00a0}1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get $y=-1.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
